<TopicPage slug="subjects/history/foundational/the-world-wars">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The two world wars are history's hinge: <strong>the empires, ideologies, borders and
    institutions of today were cast in their wreckage</strong>. They also mark the moment war
    became industrial and then existential — the first mobilising entire societies, the second
    ending with a weapon that could end civilisation. Understanding both wars is understanding
    why the post-1945 order is built the way it is.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the hinge of the twentieth century, in four frames" widget="equationStep" config={wwCfg} />

  <h2>What historians focus on</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>WWI's origins are still debated:</strong> alliances, mobilisation timetables, German ambition, Austria-Hungary's ultimatum — Fritz Fischer vs Christopher Clark's "Sleepwalkers". The debate itself teaches how causes layer.</li>
    <li><strong>The Holocaust:</strong> industrialised genocide — planned, bureaucratically organised, requiring modern state capacity. Its historiography (intentionalism vs functionalism) asks how deliberate the plan was from the start.</li>
    <li><strong>WWII was won as much by production as battle:</strong> the Allies out-produced the Axis ~3:1 in aircraft and tanks. The Eastern Front bore the brunt — ~4 of every 5 German soldiers killed died there.</li>
    <li><strong>The bomb's double lesson:</strong> Hiroshima and Nagasaki ended the war and opened the question that has defined geopolitics since — how do you deter a war you can no longer survive?</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Clark, The Sleepwalkers (2012); Snyder, Bloodlands (2010); Keegan, The Second World War.</p>
</TopicPage>
